    (0, None, '\'Nastya, why do you not eat meat?\' Ainura uses "kushat\'", a soft, homely word for eating that you\'d say to a child or at the table. The plain word is "yest\'". And the meat is "myaso".'),
    (1, None, '\'Because I am a strict vegetarian.\' "Potomu chto" is the matching answer to "pochemu": "why" and "because" make a pair. The "-ka" at the end of "vegetarian" marks a woman, so a man would be a "vegetarianets".'),
